import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Australia"
  subtitle="A branch that sailed in 1965, a Leibholz who got out in 1939, and eleven files nobody has opened"
  lede="The National Archives of Australia has this family. It has never scanned a page of it. Everything below was found in one sitting on 20 September 2026 in a free, name-indexed catalogue that this archive had never searched.">

  <h2>They came by sea</h2>
  <p>
    On 20 September 2026 this archive published that Manfred Luwinski <em>flew</em> from Berlin to
    Melbourne in 1965. Later the same day the National Archives of Australia said otherwise, and it
    said so in the title of the very document Arolsen holds the other half of.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The same roll, in two countries <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The Arolsen Archives hold an International Committee for European Migration nominal roll,
      DocID 81709354, numbered <strong>BGO 65/135</strong>, carrying the family at
      <strong>lines 85 to 88</strong>. The National Archives of Australia hold
      <strong>NAA: A2559, 1965/86/85-88</strong>. The same roll number and the same four line numbers,
      filed on opposite sides of the world by two archives that do not know of one another.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the Australian title carries what the German one does not: <strong>“arrived per ship FLAVIA
      ex Bremerhaven/Germany 17 September 1965”</strong>. They did not fly. The word <em>airplane</em>
      belongs to the Arolsen <em>folder</em> heading, and this archive read the folder instead of the
      document — <a href={base + '/method/'}>correction 9500902</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ship changes the story. Bremerhaven is not Berlin: they crossed West Germany to the North Sea
      coast, and a migrant sailing on that run took about four weeks. <strong>They left Germany in
      August 1965</strong>, and the Berlin flat was given up before that.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Four people are on those four lines. Two of them were children in 1965 and are flagged living in
    the family tree, so they are not named here — <em>although the National Archives of Australia names
    them in a public catalogue</em>. This archive's rule about the living is its own, and it is stricter
    than the record it is quoting.
  </p>

  <h2>Joachim Peter Leibholz, who reached Melbourne ten weeks before the war</h2>
  <p>
    He was born at <strong>Berlin on 20 August 1903</strong>. He landed at Melbourne from the
    <strong>Anchises</strong> on <strong>12 June 1939</strong> with his wife — Tilde on one file, Hilde
    on another — and a child the catalogue does not name. On <strong>27 July 1944</strong>, while the
    deportations were still running in the city he came from, he was naturalised at South Yarra.
  </p>
  <p>
    The naturalisation card gives his previous nationality as <strong>STATELESS</strong>. He did not
    arrive stateless; the Reich made him so after he left, and an Australian clerk wrote down the result.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And one of his files is about property inside Poland <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>NAA: A1478, EPCP63 — “German property taken over by the Polish Government -
      Leibholz J P Mr”</strong>, 1947. A claim follows at A1458, EPCH18, and German currency bonds at
      A1444, EPC164 — that last one filed under the spelling <strong>LAIBHOLZ</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our Leibholzes are from <strong>Schubin in Posen</strong>, which is Szubin in Poland. This is the
      first document found anywhere that puts a Leibholz, a Berliner and Polish property in one sentence.
      It is not proof of kinship and it is not treated as any. It is a
      <a href={base + '/lines/'}>lane of its own</a>, and the cheap tests are the Victorian death index
      and the Melbourne Chevra Kadisha, either of which would give his parents.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The eleven items, and what state they are in</h2>
  <table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Citation</th><th>What it is</th><th>Access</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A2559, 1965/86/85-88</td><td>The 1965 assisted-migrant nominal roll — 9 folios</td><td>Not yet examined</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A714, 18/8913</td><td>Leibholz naturalisation index card, 27 July 1944</td><td><strong>Open</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A435, 1944/4/1672</td><td>The naturalisation case file behind it</td><td>Not yet examined</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A12508, 21/2464</td><td>Passenger statement, <em>Anchises</em>, 12 June 1939</td><td><strong>Open</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>MP56/10, LEIBHOLZ T</td><td>“Tilde [and child]” — Melbourne</td><td><strong>Open</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A1478, EPCP63</td><td>German property taken over by the Polish Government</td><td>Not yet examined</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A1458, EPCH18</td><td>Claim for property, 1948–1956</td><td>Not yet examined</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A1444, EPC164</td><td>German currency bonds — <strong>“J P Laibholz”</strong></td><td>Not yet examined</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <strong>Not one of them is digitised.</strong> They are paper, at Canberra, Melbourne and Sydney.
    An access examination is free and is asked for by email; a copy is not. The letter is
    <a href={base + '/letters/'}>drafted and waiting to be sent</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>One spelling is not a search — and it paid twice in one sitting <span class="tag doc">Method</span></h3>
    <p>
      Eight wildcards were run: LUWINSK* · LOWINSK* · LUWENSK* · JUWINSK* · LUWWINSK* · LEIBHOL* ·
      LAIBHOL* · LEIBBHOL*.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>LAIBHOL*</strong> found a file that LEIBHOL* does not: the Australian government spelled
      its own claimant’s name differently on a different form. And <strong>LOWINSK*</strong> found a
      family that has to be kept out — Marcus William Lowinsky, who patented a cartwheel in 1892, and
      <em>Helga Lowinsky, born 14 April 1931, who sailed to Australia from Britain in 1964 and married
      a Grant</em>. The right forename, the wrong year, the wrong country. A careless search would have
      taken her for Manfred’s wife.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the search rules out, and what it does not</h2>
  <p>
    There is <strong>exactly one Luwinski item in the whole of the National Archives of Australia</strong>
    — the 1965 roll. No naturalisation file, no migrant selection document, no alien registration, under
    any spelling. That is a real finding about the index and a weak one about the records: Australian
    citizenship files after 1949 sit in series that are largely <em>not item-listed</em> in RecordSearch.
    A man who naturalised in Queensland in the 1970s can be wholly absent from this catalogue and wholly
    present in the stack. <strong>The search rules out the index, not the record.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Two other Australian instruments were tried and did not open. <strong>Trove</strong> hung and then
    began refusing this browser outright — nothing was searched there, so nothing is ruled out, and it is
    the Commonwealth Gazette rather than the newspapers that matters, because copyright closes Australian
    newspapers at 1954 and this family landed in 1965. <strong>The electoral rolls</strong> for 1965 to
    1980 are behind a subscription this archive does not buy. Both sit on the
    <a href={base + '/worklist/'}>work list</a>, marked for what they are: unopened, not empty.
  </p>

</Base>
